  "fanTake": "Well, that was an absolute catastrophe in Jacksonville. The Patriots got absolutely steamrolled 35-6—a 29-point beatdown that felt like watching someone try to play chess while their opponent was playing football. Drake Maye had the kind of day that makes you wonder about everything: three turnovers, an offense that generated -0.25 EPA per play (well below average), and a red-zone trip where New England managed to score zero touchdowns on four opportunities, the red-zone efficiency sitting at 0% against Jacksonville's horrifying 100%. Jacksonville's defense was relentless on third down too, forcing New England into a 4-of-12 (33%) conversion rate while their own offense cruised at 8-of-12. The silver lining—if you can call it that—was Marcus Jones, who led the Patriots with a +10% win probability added on defense and snagged his first interception of the season, but one player's decent afternoon doesn't fix a 29-point shellacking. This was the kind of loss that stings, the kind that forces real conversations about whether things are being done right.",
